  "answer_text": "**AltaVista.com** was one of the most popular and pioneering web search engines of the early internet era. For many who used the web in the mid-to-late 1990s, AltaVista was the gateway to the World Wide Web before Google became the dominant player.\n\n### Key Facts & History:\n\n* **The Launch (1995):** AltaVista was launched on December 15, 1995, by researchers at Digital Equipment Corporation (DEC) in Palo Alto, California. It was originally created partly as a showcase to demonstrate the immense processing power of DEC's new 64-bit Alpha computers.\n* **Immediate Success:** On its very first day, the site received over 300,000 hits. Within a couple of years, it was handling tens of millions of searches a day, making it one of the most visited websites in the world. \n* **Pioneering Technology:** \n  * **Full-text indexing:** It was the first search engine to provide a fully searchable, full-text database of a massive portion of the World Wide Web.\n  * **\"Scooter\" crawler:** It used a highly efficient, multi-threaded web crawler named \"Scooter\" to index far more pages than contemporary search engines could manage.\n  * **Babel Fish:** In 1997, AltaVista launched Babel Fish, the web\u2019s first free, real-time machine translation service.\n  * **Early CAPTCHAs:** AltaVista is credited with implementing some of the earliest CAPTCHA-like systems to prevent automated spam bots from registering accounts.\n\n### The Decline\nIn the late 1990s, AltaVista tried to replicate the success of Yahoo! by transforming its simple search bar page into a cluttered \"web portal\" filled with news, weather, email, and shopping links. This decision alienated users who preferred a clean, fast search experience. Around the same time, a new competitor emerged: Google, which offered a stark, minimalist homepage and a revolutionary PageRank algorithm that delivered far more accurate search results.\n\n### What Happened to It?\nAfter changing hands several times (going from DEC to Compaq, then CMGI, then Overture Services), AltaVista was acquired by Yahoo! in 2003. Yahoo! eventually phased out the technology behind AltaVista, using its own search database to power the site.\n\nOn **July 8, 2013**, Yahoo! officially shut down AltaVista for good. Today, typing `altavista.com` into your browser simply redirects you to Yahoo! Search.",
